Chapter: ProbeSubstringTrap
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 Finding 28. An out-of-range `substring` must KILL the program.

 Upstream ruled on this deliberately and wrote down why. Substring once took
 its start and length on trust: `substring a 0 40` on a five-byte string
 answered `abcde   s       PASSWORD-1234567890     ` -- the whole of the next
 allocation, returned verbatim. The fix traps rather than clamps, at Damian's
 ruling, "because a clamp turns a program's bug into quietly wrong data, and
 this project's virtues say a safety guarantee is never silently degraded"
 (Emit/X86_64Builtins.codex:640-666; the check is `emit-substring-bounds` at
 :666, three UD2 traps -- negative start, negative length, and `len > srclen -
 start` computed by subtraction because the obvious sum can wrap).

 `cx_substring` clamps with `@min`. So the plug takes a program that upstream
 kills and lets it run on quietly wrong data -- the exact outcome the ruling
 exists to prevent, restored by the backend that was supposed to mirror it.

 This probe is the one file here that is NOT expected to agree, and it is not
 expected to survive either. Bare metal should die on UD2 with nothing after
 the first line. The zig arm should print a length, and the length it prints
 is the size of the lie: asked for 40 bytes of a 5-byte text, it answers 5.

 Kept out of `prim-text.codex` for that reason -- a tier file that trapped
 would take its other forty assertions down with it.

Section: Main

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
   print-line-uni "-- about to ask for 40 bytes of a 5-byte text --"
   print-line-uni ("answered length: " & integer-to-text (text-length (substring "abcde" 0 40)))
   print-line-uni "-- still running, so this arm clamped rather than trapped --"
  end
